# Odoo 19.0 environment schema (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# The compose pins the official image `odoo:19.0`. On 2026-09-04 (research
# date) that tag shares its digest with `19.0-20260817` / `19` / `latest`
# (sha256:f99ffac95cb39a0924622ea4118481c95651d9c84187e5b30a21c2cc4419c7dd,
# amd64 + arm64 + ppc64le, pushed 2026-08-20). Odoo 19.0 shipped September
# 2025 and has standard support to September 2028; the docker-library
# manifest still rebuilds 17.0, 18.0 and 19.0 nightly off the same commit, so
# this is a currency move, not a rescue from an abandoned branch. Odoo 19
# raised the minimum PostgreSQL from 12 to 13; the bundled postgres:16-alpine
# satisfies that.
#
# BE CLEAR ABOUT THE CONFIG SURFACE: Odoo is configured by `odoo.conf` and
# odoo-bin CLI flags, NOT by a large environment set. There is no
# `ODOO_ADMIN_PASSWORD`, no `ODOO_WORKERS`, no `ODOO_DBFILTER`. What follows
# is the complete, honest list, from two places:
#
#   1. the official image's `entrypoint.sh` at this tag (odoo/docker,
#      commit 5930f757), which reads only HOST / PORT / USER / PASSWORD /
#      PASSWORD_FILE and turns them into `--db_host` / `--db_port` /
#      `--db_user` / `--db_password` CLI flags, and only when the matching
#      key is absent from odoo.conf;
#   2. `odoo/tools/config.py` at branch 19.0, where each option may carry an
#      `env_name`. Odoo 19 is the FIRST branch with that layer: 18.0's
#      config.py reads exactly one variable (ODOO_RC). Upstream's own 18.0
#      entrypoint says so in a comment ("Unlike 19.0, Odoo 17/18 don't read
#      PG* env vars natively"). Thirteen options have an env_name in 19.0 and
#      all thirteen are declared below. Everything else in odoo.conf
#      (admin_passwd, list_db, proxy_mode, dbfilter, workers, limit_*,
#      smtp_*) has no environment variable at any version.
#
# SECURITY: THE MASTER PASSWORD. `admin_passwd` in odoo.conf guards Odoo's
# database manager at /web/database/manager: creating, duplicating, DROPPING
# and restoring databases, plus downloading a full backup (data + filestore)
# of any database on the cluster. It is a FileOnlyOption in config.py (no CLI
# flag, no environment variable, config file only) and its built-in default
# is the literal string "admin". The image ships /etc/odoo/odoo.conf with
# `; admin_passwd = admin` commented out, so the built-in default applies.
#
# There is therefore NO env key below that sets it, and there cannot be. What
# this template does instead is make the file it lives in durable: the compose
# mounts the named volume `odoo-config` at /etc/odoo, the directory $ODOO_RC
# points into. Odoo's own controller (addons/web/controllers/database.py)
# detects the built-in default, marks the instance "insecure", and lets the
# first visitor set a master password; Odoo then hashes it and persists it by
# rewriting $ODOO_RC. With the volume that write survives every container
# recreate and redeploy. Without it (the shape this template shipped before),
# the file lived in the image layer and silently reverted to "admin" the next
# time the container was replaced. Docker seeds an empty named volume from the
# image's own /etc/odoo, so odoo.conf's `addons_path = /mnt/extra-addons` and
# `data_dir = /var/lib/odoo` come along and keep matching the other volumes.
#
# What is still on YOU: between `deploy` and your first visit, the manager
# answers to whoever reaches the port with the password "admin". Whoever gets
# there first gets to set it. Open the service yourself, set a master
# password, and create your database as the first action after deploy, or
# gate it with otterdeploy access control until you have.
#
# SECURITY: list_db. `--no-database-list` (odoo.conf `list_db = False`) is
# the real hardening switch: it hides the database list AND disables the
# manager and selector routes outright (odoo/service/db.py's
# check_db_management_enabled decorator refuses every management call). This
# template does not pass it, because with it on there is no way to create the
# first database from the UI; upstream's own help text says "be sure to set a
# proper --database parameter first". Once your database exists, adding
# `--no-database-list` (with `-d <db>`) to the service's start command is the
# single highest-value change you can make to this stack.
#
# proxy_mode. Odoo's `proxy_mode` defaults to False, which makes it build URLs
# and read the client IP from the socket rather than from X-Forwarded-*:
# http:// links in outgoing emails and in OAuth/portal redirects, and every
# request logged as the proxy's address. This template turns it on with
# `command: ["odoo", "--proxy-mode"]`. That is safe precisely because
# otterdeploy's Caddy edge always fronts the service and overwrites the
# forwarded headers; on a directly reachable port the same flag would let a
# client forge its own address, so do not carry it over to a compose that
# publishes Odoo without a proxy. Note the flag reaches Odoo intact:
# entrypoint.sh matches "odoo" in its case statement, shifts, waits for
# Postgres, and re-execs `odoo --proxy-mode` with the --db_* flags appended.
# Related: `http_interface` still defaults to 0.0.0.0 in 19.0 and upstream
# warns it becomes 127.0.0.1 in 20.0.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("Official image: environment variables", https://hub.docker.com/_/odoo)
# ---

# ─── Database connection (what the image entrypoint reads) ─────────────────

# Hostname of the Postgres server, forwarded by the entrypoint as
# `--db_host`. otterdeploy resolves the bundled db service's address; do not
# type a literal. Fallback order in entrypoint.sh is HOST, then the
# docker-link leftover DB_PORT_5432_TCP_ADDR, then the string "db".
# @required
# @docs("Odoo Docker image sources (entrypoint.sh)", https://github.com/odoo/docker)
HOST=${{stack.db.HOST}}

# Postgres port, forwarded as `--db_port`. Not set by this compose, so the
# entrypoint's own default applies (via DB_PORT_5432_TCP_PORT). Change it only
# if you repoint HOST at an external Postgres on a non-standard port.
# @type=port
PORT=5432

# Postgres role Odoo connects as, forwarded as `--db_user`. It matches the
# bundled database's POSTGRES_USER below. This role owns the cluster rather
# than one database, because Odoo creates its own databases at first run and
# needs CREATEDB. Fallbacks: USER, DB_ENV_POSTGRES_USER, POSTGRES_USER, then
# "odoo".
# @required
USER=odoo

# Password for that role, forwarded as `--db_password`. The compose threads
# the prompted POSTGRES_PASSWORD through, so the two always agree. Fallbacks:
# PASSWORD, DB_ENV_POSTGRES_PASSWORD, POSTGRES_PASSWORD, then the literal
# "odoo", which is exactly why leaving this empty is not an option.
# @required
# @sensitive
PASSWORD=${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}

# Docker-secrets variant: a path inside the container to read the database
# password FROM, instead of passing it in PASSWORD. Read first by
# entrypoint.sh, and it overwrites PASSWORD when set. Only useful if you mount
# a secret file; this template passes the value directly.
PASSWORD_FILE=

# ─── Odoo 19's native PG* layer (config.py env_name) ───────────────────────

# Same meaning as HOST, but read by Odoo itself rather than translated into a
# CLI flag, new in 19.0. Setting it makes entrypoint.sh SKIP its `--db_host`
# flag entirely, so PGHOST and HOST must not disagree. Leave empty and let
# HOST above own this. Odoo's default is an empty host, i.e. the libpq default
# (local socket).
# @docs("odoo-bin command-line and configuration reference", https://www.odoo.com/documentation/19.0/developer/reference/cli.html)
PGHOST=
# Suppresses the entrypoint's `--db_port` the same way. Empty means Odoo lets
# libpq pick 5432.
# @type=port
PGPORT=
# Suppresses the entrypoint's `--db_user`. Empty means USER above wins.
PGUSER=
# Suppresses the entrypoint's `--db_password`. Empty means PASSWORD above
# wins. Set only if you are configuring Odoo entirely through PG* variables.
# @sensitive
PGPASSWORD=

# `db_name` / `-d`: comma-separated database(s) Odoo installs or updates
# modules in, and the database it serves when the manager is disabled. Empty
# (the default) means Odoo serves whatever databases exist and shows the
# selector. Set this together with `--no-database-list` once your database
# exists.
PGDATABASE=

# `db_template`: the Postgres template new Odoo databases are cloned from.
# Odoo's default is template0, the empty one, which is why the bundled
# Postgres only needs to hand Odoo a CREATEDB role.
PGDATABASE_TEMPLATE=template0

# `db_sslmode`: libpq SSL mode for the connection to Postgres. Odoo defaults
# to "prefer". The bundled database is on the stack's private network and
# serves no TLS, so "prefer" silently falls back to plaintext there; raise it
# to require/verify-full only when you point HOST at an external Postgres that
# actually presents a certificate.
# @type=enum(disable, allow, prefer, require, verify-ca, verify-full)
# @docs("PostgreSQL: libpq environment variables", https://www.postgresql.org/docs/16/libpq-envars.html)
PGSSLMODE=prefer

# `db_app_name`: the application_name Odoo reports to Postgres, which is what
# shows up in pg_stat_activity. "{pid}" is substituted with the worker's pid;
# Odoo's default is the value below.
PGAPPNAME=odoo-{pid}

# `pg_path`: directory holding the pg_dump / pg_restore binaries Odoo shells
# out to for database backup, restore and duplicate. Empty means "found on
# PATH", correct for this image, which installs the postgresql-client
# matching the server it targets.
PGPATH=

# `db_replica_host` / `db_replica_port`: optional read replica Odoo routes
# read-only cursors to. Empty means no replica, and every query goes to HOST.
# Only meaningful with an external Postgres that has streaming replication;
# the bundled single-node database has none.
PGHOST_REPLICA=
# @type=port
PGPORT_REPLICA=

# ─── Config file and developer mode ────────────────────────────────────────

# Path to odoo.conf, where every setting that has no environment variable
# lives: admin_passwd, list_db, dbfilter, workers, limit_*, smtp_*. The image
# bakes this value and ships the file with `addons_path = /mnt/extra-addons`
# and `data_dir = /var/lib/odoo`, both matching this template's volumes. The
# directory it names, /etc/odoo, is mounted from the `odoo-config` named
# volume, which is what makes an edited odoo.conf (and the master password
# Odoo writes into it) survive a redeploy. Repointing this elsewhere opts out
# of that volume: you would then have to supply addons_path and data_dir
# yourself. The pre-Odoo-9 name OPENERP_SERVER is still detected in 19.0, only
# to emit a deprecation warning; its value is ignored.
# @docs("On-premise deployment: configuration and security", https://www.odoo.com/documentation/19.0/administration/on_premise/deploy.html)
ODOO_RC=/etc/odoo/odoo.conf

# `--dev`: comma-separated developer features (access, reload, qweb, xml, or
# "all"). Turns on source auto-reload, uncompiled assets and verbose access
# tracebacks. Never set on a deployed instance, since it leaks internals and
# disables asset caching.
ODOO_DEV=

# ─── Bundled Postgres ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The stack runs its own postgres:16-alpine on the odoo-db volume. Odoo 19
# requires PostgreSQL 13 or newer, so 16 is comfortably in range. Odoo's
# optional AI features additionally want the pgvector extension, which this
# image does not ship, so those features stay unavailable.

# Superuser-equivalent role the Postgres image creates on first boot. It is
# the same name Odoo connects as (USER above); Odoo needs CREATEDB because it
# provisions each Odoo database itself. Note that entrypoint.sh ALSO reads
# POSTGRES_USER as a fallback for USER, so the two names cannot drift.
# @required
# @docs("Official Postgres image: environment variables", https://hub.docker.com/_/postgres)
POSTGRES_USER=odoo

# Password for that role, prompted at deploy and threaded into Odoo's
# PASSWORD. Only reachable on the stack's private network, but this account
# can create and drop every Odoo database in the cluster. Generate it, never
# reuse one. `openssl rand -base64 24`.
# @required
# @sensitive
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Database the Postgres image creates on first boot. It is deliberately
# "postgres", the maintenance database: Odoo does not store anything in it and
# instead creates one database per Odoo instance through its own database
# manager. Renaming it does not rename any Odoo database.
POSTGRES_DB=postgres
